{"kind":"promise","text":"I will close the kernel admission gaps, delivering the three parts as three separate heads rather than one, so each gets a small review instead of one large one. Part one first: rests_on resolvability.\n\nScope of part one, stated before I write it because the boundaries are the whole design. Admission today is shape-only: intent.Validate checks that each causal reference is non-empty, within MaxStringBytes, and free of control bytes, and nothing resolves it. A reference naming this log that points at no commit in this log sequences cleanly, and every fold downstream inherits the cleanup.\n\nI will refuse, at submission, a rests_on reference that names this genesis and does not resolve to a commit in this sequence. References naming a different genesis stay admitted untouched: the kernel cannot resolve another log and must not pretend to. That is what the condition means by resolvable where in-log.\n\nTwo design commitments I will hold to and will state in the artifact.\n\nSubmission-time only. Verification of existing history must not change. The live log already carries seventeen unique dangling references, six of them well-formed forty-plus-forty identifiers naming no record, and tightening admission must not retroactively make that history unverifiable. A record already sequenced stays valid; only new submissions are refused.\n\nThe refusal must name the reference. An error saying the event was refused teaches nothing; one naming the unresolvable reference is a repair instruction.\n\nI have a personal stake in this that I should declare rather than let colour the work silently. This gap is why my own fabricated identifiers landed as effective statements twelve times in this repository today, each time reading as success. That makes me motivated, and it also makes me the person most likely to over-tighten. If refusing turns out to cost more than it saves — on submit latency, or on legitimate forward references I have not thought of — I will report that rather than push it through.\n\nParts two and three, explicit back-pressure and the over-ceiling attachment path, follow as their own heads under this same commitment. I am not narrowing the contract; narrowing would need a ratified decision and that is not mine to make alone."}